// Ownership of a callee-allocated value does not survive a second return.
//
// **Not named `test_*.psm`, so the runner does not execute it.** It is the
// reproduction for a known limitation, kept beside the tests because the two
// functions below differ by exactly one level of call depth and nothing else.
//
// `aif_owns_call_result_at_node` answers "does this frame own what the call
// returned". It requires the returned site to have been allocated *in the callee
// itself*:
//
//     if (sites[s].fn != c->fn) return AIF_ELEM_NONE;   // aif_support.c
//
// The guard is not arbitrary -- the comment above it is the reason: a
// pass-through leaves the value owned where it was created, and freeing it at
// the caller double-frees through whichever binding owns it there. But it also
// declines the case where the intermediate frame *returned* the value and so
// kept no claim on it, which is every ordinary producer written in Prismio.
//
// It went unnoticed while `std.string` was C: an `extern fn` carries its
// `produce` contract in the declaration, so `str_concat` answered at depth 1 and
// the question was never asked at depth 2. Moving the standard library to native
// Prismio in 2026-08 made depth 2 the common case and this reproduction the
// first thing that showed it.
//
// Under `--verify`, built with the compiler of 2026-08-26:
//
//     this file, both calls        12 allocated,  7 released,  5 leaked
//     the same file, depthTwo cut   6 allocated,  6 released,  0 leaked
//
// Every leaked allocation is one `pieceDepthTwo` result. Commenting out the
// depthTwo call is the whole difference, which is what makes this a
// reproduction rather than an observation.
//
// **The fix is not "drop the guard".** Doing that frees pass-through returns and
// double-frees them. It needs the transitive fact -- that the site escaped to
// Caller through every intermediate frame and no intermediate frame owns it --
// which is a fixed-point change, not a predicate change. See TODO.

import std.io
import std.string

// The producing runtime call is in this function's own body, so the site's `fn`
// is this function and the caller is allowed to own the result.
fn pieceDepthOne() -> String {
    return str_with_capacity(5)
}

// One level further down: the site now lives in `concat`, so the caller is
// declined and nothing ever releases it.
fn pieceDepthTwo() -> String {
    return "pie".concat("ce")
}

fn depthOne(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut s = pieceDepthOne()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        s = pieceDepthOne()
        i = i + 1
    }
    return strLength(s)
}

fn depthTwo(n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut s = pieceDepthTwo()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        s = pieceDepthTwo()
        i = i + 1
    }
    return strLength(s)
}

fn main() -> Int {
    print("depth1 ")
    println(depthOne(4))
    print("depth2 ")
    println(depthTwo(4))
    return 0
}
